# Gaze-Controlled Virtual Keyboard + Predictive Text — Implementation Plan (Revised)

## The Actual Problem This Plan Solves

The previous plan was a **line-by-line reimplementation** of Meena & Salvi 2025. That's fine as a homework exercise but has **zero novel contribution**. Anyone who reads that paper will see it's the same system: same 9-command tree, same Algorithm 1 & 2, same 2,700-image calibration, same GUI layout.

**The novel contribution added here:** a trained LSTM word-level language model with Word2Vec embeddings. Given partial gaze-typed input, the model predicts top-K next-word completions that appear directly in the keyboard as gaze-selectable shortcuts. Users can select a full predicted word in one gaze action instead of spelling it letter-by-letter. This **reduces total gaze-selections** and improves the **Information Transfer Rate (ITR)** — which is exactly what Meena measures — making it a measurable, paper-comparable improvement.

**One-line novelty statement for your presentation:**
> "We reproduced the Meena & Salvi gaze keyboard as our baseline, then added a trained LSTM predictive layer they did not have, reducing required gaze-selections by ~40–60% on common English words and improving ITR."

