#PC issues

Problem 1 — Python 3.14 is too new. Confirmed just now: TensorFlow 2.21 (the current release) does not officially support Python 3.14 — it supports 3.10 through 3.13. You'd hit install failures trying to pip install tensorflow on 3.14 as-is.

Problem 2 — bigger than the Python version, and this one would bite you even on the right Python version: TensorFlow dropped native Windows GPU support after version 2.10. Since TF 2.11, GPU training on native Windows requires either running inside WSL2 (a Linux environment inside Windows) or using a separate, less-maintained DirectML plugin. This means even if you fix the Python version, you might silently end up training on CPU only, on native Windows, with TensorFlow — exactly the "secretly not using the GPU" trap I warned about, but caused by the framework itself, not a misconfiguration you could easily catch.

My honest recommendation, given both of these stacking up: switch to PyTorch instead of TensorFlow/Keras. Reasons, not just preference:

PyTorch has native Windows GPU support with no WSL2 detour needed.
PyTorch already supports Python 3.14 as of its 2.11.0 release (March 2026).
This sidesteps both problems at once instead of fighting them separately.
Cost: the build spec we finalized used Keras-style layer syntax (Conv2D, MaxPooling2D, etc.) — translating that to PyTorch (nn.Conv2d, nn.MaxPool2d) is mechanical, not conceptually hard, and it's exactly the kind of boilerplate translation AI assistance handles well. The architecture (4 conv layers, dropout, dense, softmax) stays identical — only the syntax changes.

What I'd actually do right now, concretely:

Install Python 3.11 or 3.12 alongside your existing 3.14 (don't uninstall 3.14 — Windows lets multiple versions coexist). Get it from python.org, check "Add to PATH" during install.
Once installed, you'll have both python (whichever registered last on PATH) and can target a specific version via the Python Launcher: py -3.11 runs that specific version, py -3.11 -m venv venv creates a virtual environment pinned to it — this avoids version confusion later.
Inside that environment, install PyTorch with CUDA support (pip install torch --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu124 — matches your CUDA 12.4 driver) instead of TensorFlow.
Re-run the GPU check, PyTorch-style: import torch; print(torch.cuda.is_available()) — should print True.